==English==
{{was wotd|2011|October|18}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|la|stipulātus}}, perfect active participle of {{m|la|stipulor||I demand a guarantee}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈstɪpjuˌleɪt/|/ˈstɪpjəˌleɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-stipulate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[require]] (something) as a [[condition]] of a [[contract]] or [[agreement]].
#* 2003, Yoko Ogawa, The Housekeeper and the Professor:
#*: My contract '''stipulated''' that I would make dinner for him at six o'clock and leave at seven after finishing the dishes; but the Professor began objecting to this schedule as soon as my son arrived on the scene.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[specify]], [[promise]] or [[guarantee]] something in an agreement.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=Irene English Shoemaker|title=Van Buskirk: A Legacy from New Amsterdam
|text=Out of special love and affection they '''stipulate''' that the survivor shall remain in full possession and use the entire temporal estate which they leave behind and none of the heirs shall demand an account or proof or balance so that the survivor may manage the estate in the manner he or she thinks best.}}
# {{lb|en|US|transitive|formal|law}} To [[acknowledge]] the truth of; not to [[challenge]].
#: {{ux|en|The defense '''stipulates''' that the witness has identified my client.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2016-01-01|author=Elizabeth I. Boals|author2=Shailee D. Sharma|title=Stanton v. Armstrong: Case File|publisher=Aspen Publishers|isbn=9781601565815|page=4
|text=The parties '''stipulate''' that Exhibits 1 through 3 accompanied the initial Verified Complaint and Jury Demand, and that they are displayed in this text in the Exhibits section solely for clarity of the layout.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|followed by {{m|en|for}}}} To ask for a contractual [[term]].
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2020-03-26|author=Gaius|title=Institutes of Roman Law|publisher=Jazzybee Verlag|isbn=9783849654108
|text=Although another person cannot '''stipulate''' for us, yet in our stipulations we can associate with ourselves another person who '''stipulates''' for the same performance, and is called an adstipulator.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|formal|law}} To mutually agree.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Steven H. Bazerman|author2=Jason M. Drangel|title=Guide to Registering Trademarks|publisher=Aspen Publishers Online|isbn=9781567066838|page=22
|text=Parties '''stipulate''' to serve discovery and notice discovery depositions no later than 45 days after the opening of discovery, and to respond to discovery within 30 days and take discovery depositions no later than the closing date of discovery.}}

